index 0000000..7c8b76a
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/RSPEC_PERFORMANCE.md
@@ -0,0 +1,100 @@
+# RSpec performance notes
+
+Keep measurements scoped to one spec type and one process (`bin/rspec spec/<folder>`). Do not run the full suite or include system/streaming specs in these baselines.
+
+## Model suite - 2026-11-21
+
+Baseline and optimized runs used commit `3748cad`, random RSpec order, the local test database and Redis, and no parallelization.
+
+| Run | Command | RSpec time | Wall time | Result |
+| --- | --- | ---: | ---: | --- |
+| Baseline | `bin/rspec spec/models --format progress --profile 25` | 131.00s | 133s | 1,795 examples, 0 failures |
+| Sidekiq policy only | `bin/rspec spec/models --format progress` | 91.52s | 93s | 1,795 examples, 0 failures |
+| Initial shared fixtures | `bin/rspec spec/models --format progress` | 83.03s | 85s | 1,795 examples, 0 failures |
+| Final | `bin/rspec spec/models --format progress --seed 5729` | 79.11s | 81s | 1,795 examples, 0 failures |
+
+The final fixed-seed RSpec time is **39.6% lower** than baseline. Wall time is **39.1% lower**. Baseline and final validation both used seed `5729`; the Sidekiq-only and initial shared-fixture runs used `31554` and `19656`, respectively.
+
+### Diagnostic profile
+
+A TestProf pass was run with FactoryProf, RSpecDissect, SQL notifications, and the `sidekiq.inline` event. Profiling instrumentation adds overhead, so these values are attribution data rather than benchmark values.
+
+- 111.50s of 136.28s (81.8%) was spent in setup hooks and memoized fixtures.
+- Inline Sidekiq occupied 37.34s (27.3%) while processing 3,908 jobs.
+- Active Record notifications occupied 21.54s (15.8%) across 113,959 SQL events.
+- FactoryProf counted 4,721 factory calls. `status` (1,003), `account` (1,194), and nested `bookmark` (1,019) creation dominated the total. Times overlap for nested factories and must not be added to suite time.
+- The largest reusable setup costs were repeated status graphs in `TagFeed`, account creation in `Form::Import`, pagination notification sets in `Notification`, and the cleanup-policy status graph in `AccountStatusesCleanupPolicy`.
+
+### Model optimizations made
+
+- Model examples use Sidekiq fake mode by default. Examples that must execute an enqueued mail job synchronously use the `:inline_sidekiq` tag.
+- `TagFeed`, `Form::Import`, notification pagination, and `AccountStatusesCleanupPolicy` now use `let_it_be` for read-mostly shared records. `refind: true` gives every example a fresh Active Record instance while the records themselves are created once inside the enclosing group's transaction.
+- Example assertions, factories, and application runtime behavior were not changed.
+
+Add `:inline_sidekiq` only when an assertion genuinely depends on the worker's synchronous side effect. A job queue assertion should normally continue to use `Sidekiq.testing!(:fake)` and assert the enqueue itself.
+
+## Request suite - 2026-11-21
+
+Baseline and optimized runs use the local test database and Redis, no parallelization, and seed `5729`. The benchmark command was run once per phase against `spec/requests` only; system and streaming specs were excluded by the normal RSpec configuration.
+
+| Run | Command | RSpec time | Wall time | Result |
+| --- | --- | ---: | ---: | --- |
+| Baseline | `bin/rspec spec/requests --format progress --profile 25 --seed 5729` | 174.01s | 176.375s | 1,877 examples, 0 failures |
+| Final | `bin/rspec spec/requests --format progress --profile 25 --seed 5729` | 70.19s | 73.298s | 1,877 examples, 0 failures |
+
+The final request RSpec time is **59.7% lower** than baseline. Wall time is **58.4% lower**. Combining the fixed-seed model and request results gives 149.30s instead of 305.01s, a **2.04x speed-up** (51.1% lower total time).
+
+### Diagnostic profile
+
+A baseline TestProf pass used FactoryProf, RSpecDissect, Active Record notifications, and Sidekiq events. Profiling adds instrumentation overhead, so these values are attribution data rather than benchmark times.
+
+- 142.24s of 172.35s (82.5%) was spent in setup hooks and memoized fixtures.
+- Inline Sidekiq occupied 54.79s (31.7%) while processing 7,592 jobs. The broader Sidekiq event recorded 7,753 jobs and 58.74s.
+- Active Record notifications occupied 30.30s (17.5%) across 237,900 events.
+- FactoryProf counted 11,030 factory calls. Repeated `login_activity` (2,970), `bookmark` (1,459), `status` (1,450), `accessible_access_token` (1,166), `account` (1,070), and `user` (939) calls dominated setup. Nested times overlap and must not be added to suite time.
+- `cache_spec.rb` was the largest setup hotspot at 37.75s for 258 examples. It recreated the same account/status/poll/invite/token graph and sign-in relationships for every example. ActivityPub replies and notification APIs also repeatedly created worker-fed state per example.
+
+### Request optimizations made
+
+- Model and request examples now use Sidekiq fake mode by default. Examples that genuinely assert a synchronous worker side effect use `:inline_sidekiq`. Request context hooks set fake/inline mode before group-scoped `let_it_be` setup runs.
+- The shared API authentication context creates its user once per enclosing group with `refind: true`; tokens remain example-local because scopes and client/user bindings vary by context.
+- The cache endpoint matrix, ActivityPub reply/outbox/context fixtures, notification/conversation graphs, RSS status graphs, timeline datasets, and common admin listing fixtures now use `let_it_be` or `before_all` instead of rebuilding identical records for every example.
+- Worker-produced notification and conversation records are created once inside their tagged group while preserving the assertions that consume their synchronous effects.
+- Session-backed status authorization and Redis-populated home feeds remain example-local. Group fixtures do not persist Redis state across the suite's per-example Redis cleanup, and a login session may carry per-example identity state.
+- Fixture creation order was preserved where assertions depend on snowflake IDs and pagination links. `refind: true` is required for records mutated inside example transactions so each example receives a fresh Active Record instance.
+- Example assertions, factories, and application runtime behavior were not changed.
+
+A first request-only fake-Sidekiq pass finished in 109.86s but correctly exposed 41 examples whose setup or assertions depended on worker side effects. The tags are intentionally limited to mail delivery, unfavourite cleanup, home feeds, and notification/conversation setup rather than retaining inline mode for an entire request type.
+
+## Factory cascade - 2026-11-21
+
+The factory phase retained the fixed seed and single-process, one-folder commands. TestProf numbers below are attribution data from instrumented runs, while speed comparisons use uninstrumented runs.
+
+### Diagnostic profile
+
+- `spec/models`: 3,849 factory calls. The 706 persisted `status` records caused 705 nested bookmarks, and those bookmarks accounted for 16.54s of nested factory time. The base `user` setup also generated login activities even where no example inspected login history.
+- `spec/requests`: 4,899 factory calls. The 418 persisted `status` records caused 418 nested bookmarks, accounting for 5.31s of nested factory time. The 495 user calls generated 1,172 login-activity records, which added another 1.08s.
+- Nested factory times overlap with their parent factory times and must not be added together or used as benchmark time.
+
+### Factory optimizations made
+
+- A plain `status` no longer creates an unrelated bookmark/account graph. Specs that need the graph can use `:status_with_bookmark` explicitly.
+- A plain `user` no longer creates two login activities. Specs that need login history can use `:user_with_login_activities` explicitly or create only the records relevant to the assertion.
+- No example setup or assertion outside the fabricators changed, and no application runtime behavior changed.
+
+### Factory-phase benchmark
+
+| Run | Command | RSpec time | Wall time | Result |
+| --- | --- | ---: | ---: | --- |
+| Model pre-factory recheck | `bin/rspec spec/models --format progress --profile 25 --seed 5729` | 73.92s | 75.983s | 1,795 examples, 0 failures |
+| Model final | `bin/rspec spec/models --format progress --profile 25 --seed 5729` | 59.35s | 61.324s | 1,795 examples, 0 failures |
+| Request pre-factory documented final | `bin/rspec spec/requests --format progress --profile 25 --seed 5729` | 70.19s | 73.298s | 1,877 examples, 0 failures |
+| Request final | `bin/rspec spec/requests --format progress --profile 25 --seed 5729` | 60.38s | 62.569s | 1,877 examples, 0 failures |
+
+The model run is **19.7% faster** than its immediate pre-factory recheck and the request run is **14.0% faster** than the prior documented fixed-seed result. Together they take 119.73s, **16.9% less** than the combined 144.11s comparison baseline. Against the original 305.01s combined baseline, the two folders now run **2.55x faster** (60.7% lower RSpec time).
+
+### Next phases
+
+1. Keep future comparisons to one `spec/<folder>` command and a fixed seed. Do not infer a benchmark from a TestProf-instrumented run.
+2. Keep base fabricators limited to records required for validity. Put incidental relationship graphs in named opt-in fabricators, and periodically check that nested factory calls remain small.
+3. When sharing another fixture, verify both database and external state boundaries. Redis, file attachments, sessions, and snowflake creation order are not safely reset by a database transaction alone.
